<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  Agent-owned signing configuration (the single source of truth). Hosted on the
  root object /org/librescrs/Agent alongside Manager1. All settings are
  NON-secret; PIN/CAN/MRZ are never configuration.

  Properties are read-only over org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties; mutation goes
  through SetValue so each change is gated per-key:
    DefaultLevel / DefaultReason / DefaultLocation  -> polkit org.librescrs.agent.configure (default allow)
    TsaUrls / TslSources / CscaSources              -> polkit org.librescrs.agent.configure.trust (auth_self)
    TslCacheDir / AiaCacheDir / PluginDir           -> config-file-only (NOT settable over D-Bus; a
                                                       D-Bus-settable PluginDir is a dlopen code-exec vector)
    LastTsaUrl / CscaAnchorState                    -> read-only agent-internal state

  CscaSources rides the trust action rather than gaining one of its own: which
  country-signing anchors a passport is checked against is a trust decision of
  the same class as which trusted lists or timestamping authorities are used,
  and a fourth action would split one authorisation regime across two tables.

  ImportCscaMasterList rides that same action, and for a stronger version of the
  same reason: an import does not name a place anchors may come from, it INSTALLS
  the concrete anchors passports will be accepted or refused against. Without
  that gate any process in the user's session could quietly plant a trust store.

  The agent's config file carries three further keys that are NOT properties
  here — CscaCacheDir, Pkcs11IdleTimeoutSecs, Pkcs11MaxLifetimeSecs — all
  config-file-only. SetValue/Reset still know those names and answer
  ReadOnlyConfig; they are simply not readable over this interface.

  SetValue(key, value) rejects with:
    org.librescrs.Agent.Error.UnknownConfigKey    — no such key
    org.librescrs.Agent.Error.ReadOnlyConfig      — file-only / read-only key
    org.librescrs.Agent.Error.NotAuthorized       — polkit denied
    org.librescrs.Agent.Error.InvalidConfigValue  — wrong variant type or invalid value
  Changed(key) fires after any successful mutation (from D-Bus or agent-internal).
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.librescrs.Agent.Config1">
    <property name="DefaultLevel"    type="s"      access="read"/>
    <property name="TsaUrls"         type="as"     access="read"/>
    <property name="LastTsaUrl"      type="s"      access="read"/>
    <property name="TslSources"      type="a(sbb)" access="read"/>
    <property name="TslCacheDir"     type="s"      access="read"/>
    <property name="AiaCacheDir"     type="s"      access="read"/>
    <!-- (uri, eager) per source. Two fields, not three: a country-signing
         source names anchors, never further sources, so there is no
         list-of-lists pivot flag as TslSources' a(sbb) carries. -->
    <property name="CscaSources"     type="a(sb)"  access="read"/>
    <!-- What the agent currently believes about its country-signing anchors, for
         a client that has to have something to say. An EMPTY dict means nothing
         has been imported; there is no "unconfigured" sentinel to misread.

         RECORDED, not derived on demand: an accepted import writes this into
         the agent's configuration, so it survives a restart and a client that
         has only just connected — and therefore has no import reply to read —
         is answered from it. Changed fires for the key like any other.

         SEVERAL PUBLISHERS AT ONCE. What the ICAO directory serves is a
         collection: dozens of master lists, each published and signed by a
         different country, imported in one action. This dict was written when a
         publisher meant ONE publisher, so the three members that named one now
         speak for the whole, and each does it the same way — say what is true of
         every publisher taken in, and where the whole has no answer, be ABSENT.
         Absence is a shape this dict has always had (an undated list has always
         left signedAt out), and it is the shape a reader can act on: a client
         that finds no signer shows none, where a client handed one fingerprint
         out of twenty-eight would show a specific untruth under a label its
         dialog reads as "the publisher".

         Keys, when present:
           anchors      u  anchors held. INCLUDES CSCA link certificates, which
                           are anchors like any other, so this is not a count of
                           self-signed roots. For a collection, the UNION of what
                           the accepted lists carried, exact repeats collapsed.
           issuers      u  distinct issuing countries among those anchors.
           signer       s  lowercase hex SHA-256 over the publisher's
                           SubjectPublicKeyInfo — the fingerprint a person is
                           shown, and the value the next import from that
                           publisher is measured against. Present only while the
                           agent follows EXACTLY ONE publisher, which is every
                           single published list; ABSENT once several are
                           followed, because there is then no publisher for this
                           to name and naming one of them would be a specific
                           false statement rather than a vague one.
           signerPinned b  whether the import ESTABLISHED the identity of EVERY
                           publisher it took in — matched the fingerprint, or
                           built a path from the signer to an anchor already
                           held — rather than merely observing it. The weakest of
                           its parts, deliberately: one publisher admitted
                           unchecked among several is one whose anchors arrived
                           on a first sighting. FALSE after a
                           trust-on-first-import: a surface that renders
                           "authenticity verified" over a false here claims more
                           than was measured. ALWAYS present.
           acceptedAt   x  seconds since the epoch when the AGENT accepted the
                           file. Under no publisher's control, and not a claim
                           about any list in it.
           signedAt     x  seconds since the epoch when the list says it was
                           SIGNED, from the CMS signingTime signed attribute.
                           ABSENT when the list carries none, which CMS permits;
                           there is no zero sentinel, because a list signed at
                           the epoch and a list with no date must not read alike.
                           ABSENT for a collection too, and the two cases read
                           alike on purpose: several publishers sign at several
                           times, and no one of those is the collection's.
                           Present, like signer, only while exactly one publisher
                           is followed.
           replayRefusalActive b
                           whether a later import can be refused for rolling the
                           anchors back. ALWAYS present when anything has been
                           imported, because its FALSE is the value worth
                           knowing: it means an accepted list carried no date, so
                           "is this older than what I have" cannot be answered at
                           all. Answered for the WHOLE and, like signerPinned,
                           the weakest of its parts — one undated list among
                           twenty-eight is one publisher whose anchors can be
                           rolled back freely. A surface that stays silent leaves
                           a person unable to tell "this is safe" from "this
                           cannot be checked".
           origin       s  where the anchors came from ("import").

         WHAT THIS DICT DOES NOT SAY, so a client does not go looking for it:
         HOW MANY publishers were taken in, and how many of those were
         established. Both would be honest and neither is reported. This
         property is served from what the agent RECORDED, that record is what
         survives a restart, and it has no member able to carry a count — so
         such a key could only be present in the import reply and gone from the
         property a moment later, which is a worse answer than none. The numbers
         are logged instead.

         A CLIENT BUILT BEFORE COLLECTIONS reads exactly the keys it always
         read. No key was removed and none changed type; the three that describe
         one publisher are simply absent when there is no one publisher, and
         signerPinned answers for all of them instead of for one — never in the
         direction that would let an older dialog claim more than before. -->
    <property name="CscaAnchorState" type="a{sv}"  access="read"/>
    <property name="DefaultReason"   type="s"      access="read"/>
    <property name="DefaultLocation" type="s"      access="read"/>
    <property name="PluginDir"       type="s"      access="read"/>

    <method name="SetValue">
      <arg name="key"   type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="value" type="v" direction="in"/>
    </method>
    <method name="Reset">
      <arg name="key" type="s" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Install country-signing trust anchors from signed ICAO master lists.
         Behind polkit org.librescrs.agent.configure.trust (see above).

         A FILE IS NOT A LIST. Two shapes are taken and nothing else is guessed
         at: ONE master list as published, or the DIRECTORY EXPORT (RFC 2849
         LDIF) the ICAO Public Key Directory actually serves, which carries a
         collection — dozens of master lists, each published and signed by a
         different country. Everything below is stated per LIST: each is verified
         against its own signer, each publisher carries its own record, and the
         trust and replay rules are applied to each. The anchors installed are
         the UNION of the lists that survived all of it. What a file holds is
         decided HERE and not by the client that passed it: a client that decided
         what counts as a master list would be deciding what this agent may be
         asked to believe, and it is not the trust boundary.

         An import is a one-shot INPUT, not a source: the file a person picks may
         live in ~/Downloads and be gone tomorrow, so nothing about it is
         remembered as a place to fetch from. The accepted anchors REPLACE the
         cached set, because a master list is a complete statement of what its
         publisher vouches for and merging would keep anchors it has withdrawn.
         The same holds of the union for a collection.

         PARTIAL OUTCOMES ARE ORDINARY, not an edge. Countries publish on their
         own schedules, so a collection in which two lists are stale and
         twenty-six are current is what a person downloads on an ordinary
         Tuesday. An import takes what verifies: nothing is installed that was
         not verified and admitted, and one publisher's stale list does not deny
         a person the others. An import that admits NO list at all is a refusal
         like any other and leaves the stored anchors untouched — and a refusal
         over a collection names the reason the FIRST refused list gave, because
         ranking one publisher's failure above another's is not something this
         agent knows how to do.

         masterList is a PLAIN descriptor, not a sealed memfd. Master-list data
         is public: sealing is the agent's vocabulary for secrets, and using it
         here would say something untrue about what is being handed over. It MUST
         be a regular file (or memfd); a pipe or socket is refused, because the
         agent reads it synchronously on the bus thread and an unbounded source
         would stall every other client.

         TRUST. There is nothing to check a first list against — a master list is
         what supplies anchors — so the first accepted list establishes its
         publisher, and where there is exactly one its fingerprint is reported
         for a person to recognise. A later list must come from a publisher
         already on record, or from one that descends from an authority a
         PREVIOUS import carried. Anything else is refused: a lawful key rotation
         and an attack are indistinguishable from here, so silently accepting one
         accepts both. A list in a file may never vouch for a signer in the same
         file, whichever order they arrive in — that would be the circular check
         this design exists to avoid, one level up.

         A rotating publisher's certificate is taken from the SIGNER the incoming
         list's own verification resolved, so a client hands over the file and
         nothing else. It is never taken from the object's certificate bag, which
         is unauthenticated: anybody may drop anybody's certificate in there
         without touching a signature.

         REPLAY, and it is measured PER PUBLISHER. Once a list that carries a
         signingTime has been accepted from a publisher, a later list from that
         same publisher must be signed STRICTLY later, and one carrying no
         signingTime at all is refused. Measuring a list against another
         country's date would refuse whichever country publishes least often,
         which is not a rollback and not a threat. The asymmetry over undated
         lists is deliberate. signingTime is optional in CMS, so refusing every
         undated list would turn the feature off for a publisher that does not
         date its lists — but letting an undated list replace a dated one would
         let an attacker STRIP a protection this installation already had, and
         roll the anchors back afterwards at will. Nothing is ever refused for a
         property the ecosystem may not provide, and nothing already held is ever
         given up. Until a dated list has been accepted the refusal cannot
         operate at all, and CscaAnchorState says so through
         replayRefusalActive — for the whole, and as the weakest of its parts.

         The reply is the same dict as the CscaAnchorState property above.

         Rejections:
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.NotAuthorized              polkit denied
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.RateLimited                caller over budget
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.InvalidRequest             not a regular file, or
                                                                unreadable
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.InputTooLarge              past the size bound
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.NotAMasterList             not a master list at all
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.EmptyMasterList            signed, and carries no anchor
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.MalformedMasterList        content unreadable
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.BadMasterListSignature     signature does not hold
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.MasterListSignerChanged    a publisher the agent does
                                                                not follow. For a single
                                                                list the message carries
                                                                BOTH fingerprints, because
                                                                deciding between a rotation
                                                                and an attack is a person's
                                                                job, not the agent's; for a
                                                                collection it names
                                                                neither, since one
                                                                fingerprint out of dozens
                                                                would say something the
                                                                reason does not
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.MasterListReplayed         authentic, and not newer
                                                                than the list already
                                                                installed — older, dated
                                                                the same, or carrying no
                                                                signing time while the
                                                                installed one does. The
                                                                message carries both dates
           org.librescrs.Agent.Error.AnchorCacheNotWritable     verified, and could not be
                                                                stored — said out loud
                                                                rather than reported as a
                                                                successful import that kept
                                                                nothing -->
    <method name="ImportCscaMasterList">
      <arg name="masterList" type="h"     direction="in"/>
      <arg name="state"      type="a{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <signal name="Changed">
      <arg name="key" type="s"/>
    </signal>
  </interface>
</node>
